The CRR defines liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) as follows:

> The liquidity coverage ratio shall mean the ratio between the sum of the eligible liquid assets held by a credit institution and the amount of its net cash outflows from all sources over a one-month period, calculated in accordance with the provisions of this Regulation.

Article 2(1) of Delegated Regulation (EU) 2017/208 provides that:

> For the purposes of paragraph 1, the term 'net cash outflow' means the difference between the total inflows and outflows of cash and cash equivalents during the relevant reference period, excluding the following items:

> (a) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in securities, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(b) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (b) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in derivatives, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(c) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (c) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in other financial instruments, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(d) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (d) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in money market funds, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(e) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (e) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in repurchase agreements, reverse repurchase agreements and securities lending and borrowing transactions, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(f) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (f) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in securities financing transactions, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(g) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (g) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in securities lending and borrowing transactions, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(h) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (h) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in commodity derivatives, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(i) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (i) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in foreign exchange derivatives, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(j) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (j) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in interest rate derivatives, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(k) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (k) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in credit derivatives, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(l) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (l) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in equity derivatives, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(m) of Directive 2009/111/EC;

> (m) cash and cash equivalents received or paid for the purpose of settling transactions in other derivatives, including those which are not subject to the clearing obligation pursuant to Article 4(5)(n) of Directive 2009/111/EC.

The definition of "cash and cash equivalents" includes "all highly liquid assets that can be readily converted into cash, such as cash in hand, demand deposits with banks, marketable securities, and other short-term, highly liquid investments that are easily convertible to known amounts of cash".[1] Therefore, it is clear that settlement payments (or receipts) made in the context of derivatives structured as STM should be included in the calculation of the LCR.

[1] See https://www.investopedia.com/terms/c/cashandcashequivalents.asp

