baseline.text:
The CRR does not define what constitutes ‘liquidity inflows’. The term is left undefined in the CRR. However, the CRR refers to the definition of ‘liquidity inflows’ in the Basel III framework. This definition is found in the Basel III framework in Article 2.2.10 of the Basel III framework which states:

“Liquidity inflows” means all items included in the liquidity buffer, except for the following:

• Items that are part of the capital conservation buffer;

• Items that are part of the countercyclical capital buffer;

• Items that are part of the total loss-absorbing capacity requirement; and

• Items that are part of the net stable funding ratio requirement.”

In addition, the Basel III framework defines ‘liquidity outflows’ in Article 2.2.11 as follows:

“Liquidity outflows” means all items included in the liquidity buffer, except for the following:

• Items that are part of the capital conservation buffer;

• Items that are part of the countercyclical capital buffer;

• Items that are part of the total loss-absorbing capacity requirement; and

• Items that are part of the net stable funding ratio requirement.”

Based on these definitions, it appears that the CRR does not include any reference to the exclusion of certain types of liquidity inflows from the 75% cap. Therefore, the answer to the question is no.
